{
  "concept": "Benzene bromination proceeds through two activation barriers and a cationic intermediate, and the substitution pathway is favored over addition because its products sit lower in energy than the reactants while the addition branch ends uphill.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "pale blue rectangular panel background",
    "vertical Energy axis arrow at far left, gradient fill from pale yellow (bottom) to orange (top), labeled 'Energy'",
    "horizontal axis label 'Reaction progress' beneath the panel ending in a magenta arrowhead",
    "reactant structure: benzene hexagon + plus sign + 'Br2' in magenta text",
    "reactant dashed energy level",
    "black energy curve with two peaks separated by an intermediate trough",
    "short dashed cap line at the first peak (transition state)",
    "ΔG‡ vertical double-headed arrow between reactant level and first peak",
    "cationic intermediate structure in the trough: partly unsaturated six-membered ring with H and magenta Br bonded to the same carbon, plus sign above",
    "second, lower peak (second transition state)",
    "product dashed energy level below the reactant level",
    "ΔG° small double-headed arrow spanning the reactant-to-product gap",
    "substitution product structure: bromobenzene + plus sign + 'HBr' in magenta, labeled 'Substitution'",
    "magenta addition branch curve rising from the trough over its own maximum",
    "addition endpoint structure: bracketed dibrominated ring with two H's and two magenta Br's, labeled 'Addition' and '(does not occur)' in magenta"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "progressStep",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Reaction progress",
      "states": ["reactants", "firstTS", "intermediate", "secondTS", "products"],
      "default": "reactants",
      "teaches": "Electrophilic bromination passes through two activation barriers and a discrete cationic intermediate before forming substitution products, not a single concerted step"
    },
    {
      "id": "pathwayToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Pathway",
      "options": ["Substitution pathway", "Addition pathway"],
      "default": "Substitution pathway",
      "teaches": "Both endpoints are placed on one vertical energy scale: the substitution branch finishes downhill while the addition branch finishes uphill, which is why only substitution occurs"
    },
    {
      "id": "energyArrowClick",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "ΔG‡ measures the barrier height from reactants to a transition state (kinetics), while ΔG° measures the net gap between reactant and product levels (thermodynamics) — clicking either arrow traces its start and end levels"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Reactants",
      "narration": "Benzene and Br₂ begin on a common dashed energy level; everything that happens next plays out on the black curve above them.",
      "state": { "progressStep": "reactants", "pathwayToggle": "Substitution pathway" }
    },
    {
      "title": "First barrier",
      "narration": "The system climbs the first, tallest peak — the transition state for attacking the aromatic π system. The ΔG‡ arrow spans this full climb.",
      "state": { "progressStep": "firstTS", "pathwayToggle": "Substitution pathway" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Cationic intermediate",
      "narration": "The curve dips into a trough housing the sigma complex: a non-aromatic cation bearing both H and Br on one ring carbon. This is where the addition branch forks off.",
      "state": { "progressStep": "intermediate", "pathwayToggle": "Substitution pathway" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Second barrier",
      "narration": "A second, smaller peak must be crossed — deprotonation of the ring carbon, which restores aromaticity.",
      "state": { "progressStep": "secondTS", "pathwayToggle": "Substitution pathway" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Substitution products",
      "narration": "The black curve falls to bromobenzene + HBr on a level below the reactants: overall ΔG° is downhill, so substitution is thermodynamically favorable.",
      "state": { "progressStep": "products", "pathwayToggle": "Substitution pathway" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why addition fails",
      "narration": "Toggle to the addition pathway: the magenta branch ends on a dibrominated product level above the reactants — uphill ΔG° — so addition does not occur.",
      "state": { "progressStep": "intermediate", "pathwayToggle": "Addition pathway" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "On a single vertical energy scale the competition is decided by the endpoints: substitution loses a proton to restore aromaticity and ends downhill, while addition ends uphill and therefore does not occur.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Pale blue panel containing the full diagram. Vertical Energy arrow on the far left with a yellow-to-orange gradient; horizontal 'Reaction progress' label below with a magenta arrowhead. One black curve with two humps (first taller) and a trough; from the trough a magenta curve branches upward to the addition endpoint. All structures sit in the lower region of the panel, aligned roughly with their energy level: reactants lower-left with 'Br2' in magenta; intermediate cation centered under the trough; substitution products lower-right with 'HBr' in magenta; addition product in the upper-right enclosed in square brackets with magenta '(does not occur)'. Dashed horizontal levels mark reactants and products; a short dashed cap marks the first peak. ΔG‡ is a tall double-headed arrow, ΔG° a short one. Highlighting should light up the active structure plus the corresponding point on the curve, and the chosen pathway's curve and endpoint while dimming the other."
}

